    {
      "id": "a0",
      "pos": 1,
      "text": "The San Francisco Fed's Shapiro decomposition splits headline PCE inflation into supply-driven and demand-driven components.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a1",
      "pos": 2,
      "text": "The decomposition classifies each spending category using the sign of the comovement between unexpected price changes and unexpected quantity changes.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a2",
      "pos": 3,
      "text": "A category in which price and quantity surprises move in the same direction is classified as demand-driven.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a3",
      "pos": 4,
      "text": "Shapiro's method labels a category supply-driven when price and quantity surprises move in opposite directions.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a4",
      "pos": 5,
      "text": "In the Shapiro decomposition, a category counts as supply-driven when an unexpected price increase coincides with an unexpected decline in quantity.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a5",
      "pos": 6,
      "text": "Global supply chains for manufactured goods were disrupted during the pandemic.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a6",
