  "response": "The dispute is adjudicated by asking what prices and quantities did jointly, not by inspecting prices alone. Excess aggregate demand should push output, employment and prices up together across a broad range of markets, whereas a genuine disruption of productive capacity should raise prices in the affected markets while contracting the quantities transacted there. The evidence capable of discriminating between the two therefore consists of joint price-quantity movements, the breadth of price increases across spending categories, and the behaviour of wages and vacancies relative to goods prices.\n\nThe first formal tool in this literature is a statistical partition of the price index itself. The San Francisco Fed's Shapiro decomposition splits headline PCE inflation into supply-driven and demand-driven components. The decomposition classifies each spending category using the sign of the comovement between unexpected price changes and unexpected quantity changes, and it is that sign convention which makes a category whose price and quantity surprises move in the same direction count as demand-driven. By contrast, Shapiro's method labels a category supply-driven when price and quantity surprises move in opposite directions; that is, in the Shapiro decomposition a category counts as supply-driven when an unexpected price increase coincides with an unexpected decline in quantity.\n\nThe 2021-22 episode is contested largely because the candidate mechanisms were simultaneous rather than sequential. Global supply chains for manufactured goods were disrupted during the pandemic, or pandemic-era fiscal transfers raised household demand for goods; at least one of the two was operating, and quite plausibly both were at once. The Federal Reserve Bank of New York publishes the Global Supply Chain Pressure Index. That index reached its record high in December 2021, a reading that constitutes direct evidence of the breakdown in manufactured-goods supply chains during the pandemic.\n\nStructural modelling of the wage-price process offers a second line of adjudication. Bernanke and Blanchard estimated a system of equations for US inflation that included the vacancy-to-unemployment ratio as a measure of labor market tightness. Bernanke and Blanchard identified labor market tightness as the dominant source of the initial surge in US inflation. The Bernanke and Blanchard final report concluded that supply shocks rather than labor market tightness drove the initial surge in US inflation, a verdict for which their estimated system of equations supplies the quantitative support, and a verdict that cannot be squared with attributing the same initial surge chiefly to a tight labor market.\n\nThe decisive test, however, comes from the behaviour of the real economy during the subsequent disinflation, since demand-driven inflation can only be unwound at the cost of slack. The IMF concluded in its World Economic Outlook that global disinflation in 2023 occurred without a substantial rise in unemployment. US unemployment rose above 8 percent during the disinflation of 2023, a trajectory that cannot both hold and leave intact a picture of worldwide price deceleration achieved with no substantial increase in joblessness. Whichever of the two is retained, the logic of the test is the same: inflation that recedes while output and employment hold up was never primarily a demand phenomenon, because withdrawing excess demand requires forgone activity, while the unwinding of a supply constraint does not.",
